From f9b7ba6e51bbf7030fb7361178788c3e68e9bc5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Herman S." <429230+has207@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 21:57:30 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] [CPU] Rework lwarx/stwcx reservations onto generation
 counters

PS5X360E: ported from Xenia Edge (github.com/has207/xenia-edge) onto
Xenia Canary b083312b + PS5X360's patch. The a64 (ARM) parts are left out:
the PS5 build only uses the x64 backend.

The reservation bitmap tracked one exclusive bit per 64KB block, released
only by a matching stwcx. Three problems followed:

- Only one thread could hold a block, so a second lwarx got no reservation
  at all and its stwcx could never succeed until the first thread released.
- An abandoned lwarx leaked its bit forever. The usual CAS early-exit
  (lwarx/cmpw/bne- out) and thread teardown both do this, and afterwards
  every stwcx in that 64KB fails, so any guest CAS loop there spins forever.
- x64 emitted an unconditional int3 when an lwarx was taken with a
  reservation already live, and when a stwcx landed in a different block
  than its lwarx. PPC replaces the reservation in the first case and fails
  the store in the second.

Replace the bitmap with a hashed table of generation counters at the 128
byte Xenon granule. lwarx snapshots the address and its granule generation,
stwcx requires the flag, an exact address match and an unchanged
generation, then compare-exchanges and bumps the generation only when the
store lands. A failed stwcx leaves remote reservations alone, matching
hardware. No owned state is left to hand back, so nothing can leak and
thread teardown needs no cleanup. Table collisions and granule sharing cost
only a spurious failure, which the architecture permits.

(cherry picked from commit f0e2a16f4bc4d41ab5dbbd9ac7538b64693a24b8)
